"q-159781" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $2.1,\\,5.0,\\,4.2,\\,1.4.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =2.1+5.0+4.2+1.4 \\\\  & =12.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=4.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{4}\\,{e}^{-12.7\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6068f85b-0ea6-5161-a8d1-d4eb2504852b" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Exponential Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04cdbb19-c3e1-50e9-95b6-dba7968abaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-6.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533144} {:answer/id #uuid "09db5eb7-a0d5-5003-ad1f-55526d2c8fd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-5}\\,{e}^{7.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533157} {:answer/id #uuid "4d2a5e69-fccf-5474-9c74-f9ae4d8263eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{-6.5}\\,{e}^{5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533325} {:answer/id #uuid "c55c269b-4229-5d74-ac99-7a306e45927c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{7.5}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533614} {:answer/id #uuid "eaf9077c-1504-5150-89a1-24c8262596a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eaf9077c-1504-5150-89a1-24c8262596a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533719} :answer-field/id #uuid "66bb6848-6df8-5544-91de-3216e134df6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0422034f-fdb1-53c7-b35d-c57baa06d2e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-159886" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Exp}(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $0.6,\\,1.8,\\,0.7,\\,2.9,\\,1.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent exponential random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\theta {e}^{-\\theta x},\\,x\\ge 0.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\theta {e}^{-\\theta {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{n}\\,{e}^{-S\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0.6+1.8+0.7+2.9+1.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{5}\\,{e}^{-7.5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186533974 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c10a342-33a3-5012-8a4e-3198139f6867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533321} {:answer/id #uuid "5b4ba814-6782-55d8-96db-e50161653b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.6+5.2\\theta -6{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533347} {:answer/id #uuid "b40ee415-1e01-58ec-a195-7fc6ee04552c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.3-2.6\\theta +3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533577} {:answer/id #uuid "c5536e13-2ef3-5e73-85a5-44744385cc72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11.3+2.6\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533613} {:answer/id #uuid "ff9e2870-85b7-5a1c-951e-e909b21fa61b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.3-5.2\\theta +3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c10a342-33a3-5012-8a4e-3198139f6867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533321} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f4b3a32-9b08-55e4-95ee-7361270b62bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0731a97b-5085-5ebd-87e8-36cd1dc34ba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-159952" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean. For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{6},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}=5.2,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=5.2,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=22.6,$ and $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(22.6-2(5.2)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-11.3\\,+\\,5.2\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-11.3+5.2\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0681314e-d960-5bb5-a4e3-5fe913bb01ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.2-16.6\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533150} {:answer/id #uuid "0f708f9a-916c-5539-8d5f-bfdabc63a213" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13.2-16.6\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533177} {:answer/id #uuid "790804e9-daa4-5fb8-bde3-59169ded9144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33.2+13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533426} {:answer/id #uuid "dc99796b-4535-56e1-be58-6cc8406c3823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16.6-13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533682} {:answer/id #uuid "ddb41dee-3cc0-53a3-9343-0f5c275f0862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16.6+13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc99796b-4535-56e1-be58-6cc8406c3823" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16.6-13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533682} :answer-field/id #uuid "b697133a-5977-5733-b1c6-65040dd5324d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533912}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b81cde63-dfd8-59f4-90cc-c6262f4bcfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-159954" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}=-13.2,\\,{\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}^{2}=33.2.\n$$\n\nGiven that the likelihood function of the sample is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{256{\\pi }^{8}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$\n\n*Hint: The probability density function for a normal random variable with mean $\\theta $ and variance $1$ is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=-13.2,$ $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}=33.2,$ and $n=16.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-16/2}\\,{e}^{-(33.2-2(-13.2)\\theta +16{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{256{\\pi }^{8}}\\,{e}^{\\,-16.6\\,-\\,13.2\\theta \\,-\\,8{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-16.6-13.2\\theta -8{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192684 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d363c839-34b6-55c7-85d8-f2013e08cc62" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3171f3f1-b42e-5b23-bf4a-d4abdcfda312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8.92+12\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533260} {:answer/id #uuid "36743fa8-d11f-5277-bb11-6800921de937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10.04+6\\theta -{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533267} {:answer/id #uuid "44588364-77e7-5293-9871-f77f9b19a264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10.54+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533298} {:answer/id #uuid "63fa5ce6-de9e-5d28-b5ef-e762a11c1eb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.88+12\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533368} {:answer/id #uuid "a04280ae-ca06-57f0-95b1-5cb9b2387e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8.12+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44588364-77e7-5293-9871-f77f9b19a264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10.54+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533298} :answer-field/id #uuid "13f209dd-c051-5b5e-8018-eed49751c9a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186533793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf2980b5-8d36-5df9-841e-821c9785b285" :question/math-academy-id "q-159956" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4},{X}_{5},{X}_{6}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼N(\\theta ,1),$  where $\\theta $ is an unknown mean.  Given that the likelihood function of the sample $1.7,\\,2.3,\\,2.2,\\,1.1,\\,1.9,\\,1.8$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,g(\\theta )},\n$$\n\nwhat is the expression for $g(\\theta )?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed continuous random variables with probability density function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=6$ independent normal random variables with unknown mean $\\theta $ and variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=1,$ for which the probability density function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{x-\\theta }{\\sigma })}^{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-(x-\\theta )^{2}/2},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi }}{e}^{-({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2}) \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-\\sum ({x}_{i}-\\theta )^{2}/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(\\sum {x}_{i}^{2}-2\\theta \\sum {x}_{i}+n{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =(2\\pi )^{-n/2}\\,{e}^{-(C-2S\\theta +n{\\theta }^{2})/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $C={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =1.7+2.3+2.2+1.1+1.9+1.8 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ C & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =(1.7)^{2}+(2.3)^{2}+(2.2)^{2}+(1.1)^{2}+(1.9)^{2}+(1.8)^{2} \\\\  & =21.08\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $n=6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =(2\\pi )^{-6/2}\\,{e}^{-(21.08-2(11)\\theta +6{\\theta }^{2})/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8{\\pi }^{3}}\\,{e}^{\\,-10.54\\,+\\,11\\theta \\,-\\,3{\\theta }^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(\\theta )=-10.54+11\\theta -3{\\theta }^{2}.\n$$"}]}
